75
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:14,400
You know, we humans can only click so many times in terms of a button.

76
00:12:14,542 --> 00:12:16,880
And hopefully some of us are sleeping.

77
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:19,844
so we're not working during that time when we are sleeping.

78
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:26,240
But imagine if every human had at least 10 plus agents, and these agents don't sleep, they work 24-7.

79
00:12:26,702 --> 00:12:30,160
Now imagine the kind of scale that you're talking about with agents.

80
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:38,480
And these agentic workloads are dynamic, they're bursty, they're spiky, and it's really hard to be able to scale our regular systems with it.

81
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:42,212
And this is something that we have been thinking about, we have been innovating about.

82
00:12:43,022 --> 00:12:44,932
Now, orchestration itself.

83
00:12:45,294 --> 00:12:51,588
Now, most of you are moving from an individual contributor to orchestrating these agents, being a manager of these agents.

84
00:12:52,014 --> 00:12:56,420
So when agents are handling the heavy lifting, what is the human value that you can provide?

85
00:12:56,686 --> 00:13:04,068
