00:33:50,032 --> 00:33:50,832
And hello, everyone.

280
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:56,272
As Sujata said, I'm sorry I can't be there in person, But I look forward to speaking with you virtually.

281
00:33:56,462 --> 00:33:57,232
I'm Andrew Brook.

282
00:33:57,472 --> 00:34:00,260
I'm an engineering director for Google's Agentic Data Cloud.

283
00:34:01,102 --> 00:34:06,980
Um I'm sure I don't need to convince you that nearly every developer is building agents today.

284
00:34:07,392 --> 00:34:13,444
those agents need to read and write data in databases and data warehouses to automate real business workflows.

285
00:34:14,030 --> 00:34:24,132
What I hope to share is how you can build some production application agents that are grounded in your data and the engineering patterns and tools that make them accurate, secure, and fast.

286
00:34:26,893 --> 00:34:33,476
Now, when our customers build production data agents today, they tell us they run into three walls, often in the order that you see here.

287
00:34:33,856 --> 00:34:35,235
The first is accuracy.

288
00:34:35,616 --> 00:34:46,724
If an LLM writes a logically wrong query, and as a result, it tells the customer that's the wrong status of their order or puts them on the wrong flight, the trust is lost.

289
00:34:47,136 --> 00:34:51,056
That accuracy is what earns your agent a chance to operate in production.

290
00:34:51,136 --> 00:34:54,275
And until you can achieve it, production really isn't an option.

291
00:34:55,886 --> 00:35:06,500
But before you can even deploy your agent into production, even if it's accurate, you still need to guarantee that the agent can't leak another user's rows or change data it wasn't supposed to be able to touch.

292
00:35:07,536 --> 00:35:11,236
And we all know that prompt guidelines simply aren't enough for really sensitive data.

293
00:35:11,534 --> 00:35:13,252
So you have to address security.

294
00:35:13,902 --> 00:35:20,644
And then when you've launched your pilot, you've gotten an agent that works reliably, you've addressed the security concerns, you have a pilot up and running.

295
00:35:21,056 --> 00:35:25,924
We often hear that performance is disappointing, latency is high, the bill is sometimes high.

296
00:35:27,856 --> 00:35:34,852
Observe the agents going through these endless schema discovery and retry loops burns through your token budgets, and keeps your users waiting.

297
00:35:35,216 --> 00:35:39,652
So before you can really deploy an agent at scale, you have to address efficiency.

298
00:35:41,646 --> 00:35:48,708
And what I'm happy to tell you that there is a small set of principles, and really one in particular, that address a lot of these issues.

299
00:35:49,198 --> 00:35:55,876
And that really is that you don't ask the LLM to improvise or invent stuff on the fly that could be made deterministic.

300
00:35:56,558 --> 00:36:09,988
So in the case of accuracy, we want to constrain the LLM to using pre-written verified template queries where we can, and to ground generation in curated context where a query template isn't an option.

301
00:36:10,416 --> 00:36:15,456
And of course, we have to measure both of these with a series of evaluations to ensure accuracy.

302
00:36:15,886 --> 00:36:23,748
On the security side, we never take any security critical parameters like user identity from an agent, from the LLM itself.

303
00:36:24,336 --> 00:36:33,536
Instead, we want to bind it deterministically from a verified auth token or something that comes through via the tool from the application layer.

304
00:36:33,616 --> 00:36:36,656
So it's set by the application itself, it's not set by the agent.

305
00:36:36,942 --> 00:36:44,388
Or it's something that we can verify and bind at the database layer through a special kind of database view that I'll talk about in a moment.

306
00:36:45,358 --> 00:36:54,468
And for efficiency, templates and curated context already help turn some of those multi-step trial and error loops into a single fast pass execution.

307
00:36:56,974 --> 00:36:58,976
Now we deliver this pattern through two products.

308
00:36:59,456 --> 00:37:02,404
One is called MCP Toolbox and the other is called Query Data.

309
00:37:02,958 --> 00:37:05,136
Their capabilities overlap intentionally.

310
00:37:05,376 --> 00:37:07,780
And so of course that raises the question of how do you choose?

311
00:37:08,336 --> 00:37:11,140
And it really depends on your use case and your development process.

312
00:37:11,822 --> 00:37:15,844
Now, MCP Toolbox, in that case, you write every query as code.

313
00:37:16,176 --> 00:37:23,296
Every tool is defined in configuration, typically in a like a tools.yaml file or something.

314
00:37:23,696 --> 00:37:26,276
And it's a they're all parameterized SQL statements.

315
00:37:26,896 --> 00:37:34,084
So if your agent needs to do writes and transactions, if it connects out to third-party databases, you can do all of that through Toolbox.

316
00:37:34,680 --> 00:37:40,164
It's great for operational use cases where your agent needs to focus on specific workflows and actions.

317
00:37:41,134 --> 00:37:43,492
With query data, you curate context.

318
00:37:43,856 --> 00:38:03,012
it's read-only, it's built for open-ended or ambiguous questions at larger scale, and it really addresses cases where templates can cover the common shapes and context that is derived from templates but from other sources as well, can ground the long tail.

319
00:38:03,470 --> 00:38:07,616
And we give you a set of built in eval capabilities to

320
00:38:10,926 --> 00:38:14,692
help your agent to help you through the coding process to tune that accuracy.

321
00:38:17,198 --> 00:38:18,532
Now, let's start with MCP.

322
00:38:19,278 --> 00:38:28,100
Now, on the left hand side, you can see that Google provides some fully managed remote MCP servers with general purpose tools like Execute SQL or Create Instance.

323
00:38:28,736 --> 00:38:36,176
these are great for coding assistance, they're good for database administration and other situations where you have a trusted human in the loop.

324
00:38:36,576 --> 00:38:40,996
But today's talk, as I mentioned earlier, is really about building production application agents.

325
00:38:41,616 --> 00:38:45,536
in production, you can't hand an autonomous agent raw access to the database.

326
00:38:45,776 --> 00:38:49,412
That's why we build MCP Toolbox for databases, which is on the right.

327
00:38:50,096 --> 00:38:54,020
It's an open source framework that has over 16,000 stars on GitHub.

328
00:38:54,656 --> 00:39:05,764
It lets you expose dozens of constrained custom SQL tools across different Google Cloud database services, as well as a large list of third-party databases.

329
00:39:08,174 --> 00:39:10,816
And here's what writing your queries as code looks like.

330
00:39:11,056 --> 00:39:13,604
In this case, it's for a fictional airline called SymbolAir.

331
00:39:14,336 --> 00:39:17,216
The tools are declared in just simple configuration files.

332
00:39:17,696 --> 00:39:18,576
They're YAML files.

333
00:39:18,656 --> 00:39:19,908
You see a couple instances here.

334
00:39:20,398 --> 00:39:23,696
On the left, we declare our alloy db and big query connections.

335
00:39:23,950 --> 00:39:26,276
The credentials are locked away in Secret Manager.

336
00:39:26,958 --> 00:39:29,216
On the right, we define a parameterized tool.

337
00:39:29,376 --> 00:39:32,068
In this case, it's a search symbolair flights tool.

338
00:39:32,590 --> 00:39:35,136
And the LLM doesn't need to write that SQL.

339
00:39:35,376 --> 00:39:40,292
At runtime, it just needs to pick the values for variable one and variable two.

340
00:39:41,230 --> 00:39:50,244
And for security, toolbox supports authenticated parameters, which allow you to bind a parameter like the user identity directly to the user's verified ID token.

341
00:39:51,056 --> 00:39:55,456
So that that value comes from the OAuth token itself, never from the LLM.

342
00:39:55,536 --> 00:39:59,940
There's no way for the LLM to uh you know to change the identity or to spoof the identity.

343
00:40:00,526 --> 00:40:02,660
And all of this can be done with just a few lines of YAML.

344
00:40:05,934 --> 00:40:07,776
So let's see if I can get this to play.

345
00:40:08,206 --> 00:40:09,476
If I can, I'll show you.

346
00:40:13,070 --> 00:40:13,572
Here we go.

347
00:40:13,856 --> 00:40:24,868
So we're looking at Gemini Enterprise, and we're going to simulate the behavior of a travel agent working with a customer to book a flight, in this case from San Francisco to LA.

348
00:40:28,686 --> 00:40:32,484
So you first ask if there are any flights from SFO, this was from August 30th.

349
00:40:33,134 --> 00:40:34,576
It's going to pull up a custom tool.

350
00:40:34,766 --> 00:40:37,028
In this case, it's that search symbol air flights tool.

351
00:40:37,518 --> 00:40:43,844
That allows it to immediately find different flight options, return those values, and give you back a confident answer.

352
00:40:45,934 --> 00:40:53,988
So now the user can select one of those flights, and they want to look up information about a particular user, in this case, John Doe.

353
00:40:58,606 --> 00:41:04,336
And again, we're going to use those tools, the get customer ID tool and the search seats by flight tool.

354
00:41:04,416 --> 00:41:05,696
Again, these are all predefined tools.

355
00:41:05,856 --> 00:41:07,396
The agent's not writing this at runtime.

356
00:41:08,206 --> 00:41:15,428
It's going to pull up sensitive information like the customer identity that gets linked to from the uh from auth information.

357
00:41:16,174 --> 00:41:19,588
It's acknowledged by the uh the travel agent.

358
00:41:21,230 --> 00:41:23,376
And then finally it goes to the process of booking the flight.

359
00:41:23,456 --> 00:41:29,444
And again, because this is a sensitive operation, it goes through and asks for final acknowledgement from the user.

360
00:41:31,310 --> 00:41:32,932
And that completes the ticket reservation.

361
00:41:35,150 --> 00:41:36,356
Let's move on to the next one.

362
00:41:39,022 --> 00:41:49,508
So, as you can see, that works pretty well for a defined use case where you have a known sequence of steps and a relatively constrained set of questions that you might need to ask from your data.

363
00:41:50,254 --> 00:41:56,100
Um, and so you know, writing every query and toolbox like that is ideal when you know those exact operations up front.

364
00:41:56,750 --> 00:42:02,276
But what happens when users ask lots of open-ended multi-table questions like this one?

365
00:42:02,816 --> 00:42:11,364
they say find all customers in California who bought a winter code in July and returned it within 14 days and grouped them by marketing campaign that originally acquired them.

366
00:42:12,046 --> 00:42:14,576
There's really no way you can anticipate that question in advance.

367
00:42:14,816 --> 00:42:19,332
you can't pre write a dedicated tool for every possible combination of filters, joins, and groupings.

368
00:42:19,790 --> 00:42:21,156
And that's where Query data comes in.

369
00:42:23,086 --> 00:42:32,260
So, of course, you might be asking the logical question of how does query data address this without falling into the same traps of just having an LLM generate SQL on its own?

370
00:42:33,006 --> 00:42:38,724
And it does that by following that core rule that I mentioned before, which is that we constrain where we can and we ground where we can't.

371
00:42:39,534 --> 00:42:47,492
So, specifically, what this means is that instead of writing one-off tools, you curate three things in context that comes from knowledge catalog.

372
00:42:48,256 --> 00:42:50,736
One of these is the schema ontology.

373
00:42:50,896 --> 00:42:55,012
So you have a rich set of descriptions of tables, columns, and relationships.

374
00:42:55,536 --> 00:43:05,092
you build up a model that grounds sort of explains how your data is structured, and that grounds the generation when a question doesn't match an existing template.

375
00:43:05,806 --> 00:43:10,256
But in the cases where you can produce templates, you produce these things, we call them query blueprints.

376
00:43:10,336 --> 00:43:12,100
They're a combination of templates and facets.

377
00:43:12,718 --> 00:43:24,068
They're pre-verified SQL templates, very similar to what you saw in Toolbox, but they also come with modular query fragments that can constrain different common question patterns.

378
00:43:25,102 --> 00:43:37,216
And finally, we have value searches, which is a combination of some specialized models and some text and vector lookups that allow us to very quickly figure out, you know, that in this case, you know, you know, what does winter code refer to?

379
00:43:37,296 --> 00:43:42,096
You know, how is that referenced in the database, or um, you know, how is California referenced in the database?

380
00:43:42,336 --> 00:43:46,980
you can map those uh those value phrases into the exact literals that are used in your database.

381
00:43:49,102 --> 00:43:51,684
Now, of course, we don't ask you to do this all by yourself.

382
00:43:52,576 --> 00:43:54,372
you do this with the help of agents.

383
00:43:54,894 --> 00:43:57,604
Specifically, you go through the following process.

384
00:43:58,542 --> 00:44:04,096
you do context preparation, you use a developer extension in your coding agent.

385
00:44:04,206 --> 00:44:07,616
Now that could be anti-gravity, it could be cloud code, it could be codex.

386
00:44:07,776 --> 00:44:08,516
We support several.

387
00:44:09,376 --> 00:44:12,836
and you use that to generate the context set and store it in knowledge catalog.

388
00:44:13,616 --> 00:44:19,056
Then you go through an evaluation loop where your coding agent triggers uh eval bench runs.

389
00:44:19,246 --> 00:44:22,532
Evalbench is our open source framework for doing evaluations of agents.

390
00:44:23,296 --> 00:44:28,484
you do multiple runs with evalbench to measure accuracy, and you iteratively close those gaps.

391
00:44:28,576 --> 00:44:33,956
So you find all the cases where the agent wasn't able to answer something correctly and you make adjustments to the context.

392
00:44:34,894 --> 00:44:44,292
And then finally, when you're in production, you promote that verified content that allows you to push those accuracy improvements out to your running agent without redeploying code.

393
00:44:44,686 --> 00:44:51,716
And you can build feedback loops where if users are asking questions that aren't well covered, those can be pushed back into that verification loop.

394
00:44:54,414 --> 00:45:02,404
So here I'll show you just a quick example of that, how that loop looks when we went through an 18-table schema.

395
00:45:02,574 --> 00:45:05,540
We had set up an eval data set with 63 questions.

396
00:45:06,318 --> 00:45:11,936
Now, if you start at the top with just a bare schema, no context, the accuracy is not very good.

397
00:45:12,016 --> 00:45:14,496
It's about 79%, you know, almost 80%.

398
00:45:15,054 --> 00:45:17,764
Uh, and the median latency is about three and a half seconds.

399
00:45:18,606 --> 00:45:24,260
But as you watch across each iteration, we did five iterations in this example.

400
00:45:26,016 --> 00:45:44,452
the context engineering and evals, they added some templates, they added some facets, they set up some value searches using the trigram extension in Postgres, and that accuracy climbed to 100% on the eval data set, and the median latency dropped more than three times to just over one second.

401
00:45:45,358 --> 00:46:00,900
Uh and the the reason this is happening is when you look at that blueprint hit column, I think it's the second numeric column, that bottom row got up to somewhere between 85 and 87 percent of questions are now hitting a template.

402
00:46:01,582 --> 00:46:10,884
Um and while the remaining 13% are being answered accurately because they have enough grounding from the remaining context.

403
00:46:11,822 --> 00:46:18,096
So again, you constrain to a template where you can and you ground with all this rich context where you can't.

404
00:46:18,256 --> 00:46:25,028
The combination of those two allows you to build very high accuracy and to optimize for performance.

405
00:46:27,758 --> 00:46:30,928
So we're going to see both of those models working together.

406
00:46:31,248 --> 00:46:34,052
This time it's going to be in the context of a real estate portal.

407
00:46:34,830 --> 00:46:39,568
And so first I'm going to show a standard property search working.

408
00:46:40,494 --> 00:46:48,528
Query Data is an NL2 SQL tool that streamlines schema discovery, query generation, query execution, and answer generation.

409
00:46:48,846 --> 00:46:55,140
Out of the box, query data provides better token cost and latency than relying on agentic orchestration of these operations.

410
00:46:55,470 --> 00:47:00,368
When you add context about your application, it is able to get to near 100% accuracy.

411
00:47:00,622 --> 00:47:05,220
When we ask a complex question, unguided generation hits an accuracy ceiling.

