439
00:49:25,166 --> 00:49:33,124
Now finally, let's come back to security and the principle that I mentioned earlier that identity or other security-sensitive parameters should never come through the LLM.

440
00:49:33,968 --> 00:49:38,340
earlier we saw how MCP toolbox binds authenticated parameters at the tool layer.

441
00:49:38,926 --> 00:49:43,236
But what if you want deterministic access control enforced directly inside the database?

442
00:49:43,758 --> 00:49:50,212
Uh so that way you can have that control apply whether the query comes from toolbox or it comes from query data or it comes from some other tool.

443
00:49:50,768 --> 00:49:53,568
And that's what a parameterized secure view does.

444
00:49:53,774 --> 00:49:54,596
So a PSV.

445
00:49:55,054 --> 00:49:58,528
A PSV is a view that is parameterized by variables.

446
00:49:58,670 --> 00:50:03,204
Now it could be a variable like the currently logged in user or their email address, it could be other variables.

447
00:50:03,968 --> 00:50:12,048
but it's parameterized by one of those variables that is set at runtime by the application and it comes from verified sources.

448
00:50:12,128 --> 00:50:15,968
So it could be again from a user credential or something like that.

449
00:50:16,078 --> 00:50:17,764
It is not passed in by the agent.

450
00:50:18,286 --> 00:50:28,548
So the agent is only granted access to the view, and that view only contains the information that is specified in the predicate of that view that takes into account that parameter.

451
00:50:29,294 --> 00:50:32,048
Uh so the agent doesn't get to see the underlying table.

452
00:50:32,208 --> 00:50:41,168
So in this particular case, I have an example from a ticketing application where we have some payments information, and it doesn't matter.

453
00:50:41,248 --> 00:50:45,540
you know, what the, what question the user asks or how you trick the LLM?

454
00:50:46,062 --> 00:50:52,100
the actual view is constructed using the predicate that you see here.

455
00:50:54,448 --> 00:51:08,740
Um and in particular, it has a where clause that says where user ID equals current user, and that current user, this value, is passed in by the application and is taken directly out of their security, out of the credentials of the logged in user.

456
00:51:09,390 --> 00:51:10,756
So there's no way to get around it.

457
00:51:10,848 --> 00:51:14,128
And that view now only contains information about the logged in user.

458
00:51:14,222 --> 00:51:19,780
If they ask questions about another user or ask for information they're not supposed to see, the agent simply doesn't have access to it.

459
00:51:23,150 --> 00:51:33,124
So to wrap up, I hope I've showed how you can bring your agents to production grounded in your enterprise data and ready to operate reliably, securely, and efficiently at scale.

460
00:51:34,094 --> 00:51:48,100
Now, in particular, the first principle is that production data agents succeed when you constrain the LLM by using verified query templates where you can and grounding their generation in curated context where they need to generate something.

461
00:51:49,294 --> 00:51:54,756
The second principle is just never taking security-sensitive parameters like identity through the LLM.

462
00:51:55,310 --> 00:52:07,088
You want that information to come, you want it to be bound deterministically with some kind of a trusted source, like an authenticated parameter from an auth provider or something that is set at the application layer.

463
00:52:07,246 --> 00:52:11,328
So the agent can't, the LLM can't change it.

464
00:52:11,968 --> 00:52:15,748
or you can enforce it at the database layer with a parameterized secure view.

465
00:52:16,814 --> 00:52:21,412
Uh and of course, it probably should go without saying, but there are no silver bullets here.

466
00:52:21,870 --> 00:52:26,532
Quality and performance are a function of iterative tuning and optimization.

467
00:52:27,054 --> 00:52:34,340
We provide a set of tools, skills, and an evaluation framework to help you to automate this process, but you do have to go through this process.

