688
01:14:03,790 --> 01:14:12,580
So, some fundamental tenets, some non-negotiable architectural principles that databases need to follow are one, you need safe access to real-time data.

689
01:14:13,230 --> 01:14:16,932
So if you want real-time intelligence, you can't feed your agents stale data.

690
01:14:17,232 --> 01:14:26,532
So that's like um a must-have in order to get the latest operational intelligence to your agents so that they can actually give you timely decisions.

691
01:14:27,246 --> 01:14:28,644
The second thing is scalability.

692
01:14:29,454 --> 01:14:36,112
So agents don't work in a predictable curve, I mean, they don't arrive in a predictable curve like customers at a store.

693
01:14:36,192 --> 01:14:47,460
Instead, they come in massive swarms, they come in bursty workloads, which means that you need a system that can instantly scale up to thousands of agents and then immediately be able to elastically shrink back.

694
01:14:48,206 --> 01:14:51,632
And that also means that you need a system that can be cost effective.

695
01:14:51,712 --> 01:14:58,084
You cannot, simply provision enough headroom for your largest agent traffic that would just be killing.

696
01:14:58,734 --> 01:15:01,028
Finally, you need an environment that is safe.

697
01:15:01,552 --> 01:15:08,672
so you need an isolated environment where the agents can read the data, get the latest information, but only read the data that they are allowed to read.

698
01:15:08,814 --> 01:15:10,372
So you have the guardrails in place.

