02:22:19,088 --> 02:22:20,884
We typically call it tools.yaml.

1416
02:22:21,648 --> 02:22:30,020
we define everything that's required for this database sources, parameterized SQL queries, and tool groups, as we'll see going forward.

1417
02:22:30,368 --> 02:22:34,848
And also we provide a nice operational decoupling.

1418
02:22:35,022 --> 02:22:53,380
So, for instance, you can imagine a database a DBA doing SQL query amendments or you know, tuning their database indexes and rotating credentials separately from the actual agentic application logic.

1419
02:22:55,088 --> 02:23:12,288
So MCP Toolbox sits right between the agents and the databases, and it's completely open source, and we have a growing healthy community around it, 16.8 K plus stars, a huge amount of contributors.

1420
02:23:12,528 --> 02:23:20,048
And we have it customizable and also in a very out-of-the-box way.

1421
02:23:20,208 --> 02:23:33,328
So for instance, if you want to have something running quickly on your databases, you can have, you can take advantage of our pre-built tools, which take care of all the common operations across all the databases that we support.

1422
02:23:33,518 --> 02:23:42,756
Or if you want something deeper, you could easily configure specific SQL queries to narrow access on your tools.

1423
02:23:44,368 --> 02:23:54,448
and then of course, as we'll see forward, we have uh secure connection management and also we take care of optimizations like connection pooling and stuff.

1424
02:23:55,888 --> 02:24:02,564
so we have two important questions while connecting agents to databases.

1425
02:24:02,928 --> 02:24:11,008
And the first question is how do we control what an agent can do or run as a SQL query on your databases?

1426
02:24:11,182 --> 02:24:15,940
And the second one is control what the agent actually can see on your databases.

1427
02:24:16,128 --> 02:24:17,488
Both of them are important.

1428
02:24:17,648 --> 02:24:19,168
Let's tackle the first one.

1429
02:24:19,502 --> 02:24:22,928
We have four controls in MCP toolbox built in.

1430
02:24:23,118 --> 02:24:24,452
First is read-only mode.

1431
02:24:25,168 --> 02:24:31,008
one setting enabled on AlloyDB database can enable the read-only mode.

1432
02:24:31,168 --> 02:24:42,180
Your agents that may answer questions about stocks cannot write on a database at all.

1433
02:24:43,054 --> 02:24:49,860
And we enable this read-only mode on the AlloyDB database engine through MCB toolbox through just one simple setting.

1434
02:24:50,208 --> 02:25:01,316
And what that means is even with prompt injection and all the clever tricks that have been coming up, your agent can't run a delete or drop query on the database engine.

1435
02:25:02,608 --> 02:25:04,612
next we have parameterized tools.

1436
02:25:05,008 --> 02:25:12,612
So your agent really writes a full SQL query for most use cases.

1437
02:25:12,928 --> 02:25:16,388
And for that, we have secure parameterized tools.

1438
02:25:17,088 --> 02:25:35,588
you write the entire SQL query with parameters or potholes in them, and your agent chooses one of those and just takes the parameters from the user and plugs in and runs the SQL query in a very secure way and a very predictable way, which is very important.

1439
02:25:36,110 --> 02:25:37,968
Then we have groups for each agent.

1440
02:25:38,128 --> 02:25:52,288
So let's say an agent that is only maintaining stocks of inventory, they could only get those tools and not the billing or pricing tools.

1441
02:25:52,718 --> 02:25:56,288
Then we have input validation, which we'll see going forward.

1442
02:25:56,608 --> 02:26:04,004
we can set allowed values and ranges for numeric values, which makes all the more difference on the security aspect.

1443
02:26:06,190 --> 02:26:10,212
next question is how do we control what the agent sees?

1444
02:26:10,528 --> 02:26:14,564
And for that, we have again four more controls.

1445
02:26:14,926 --> 02:26:17,508
The most important one is authorized invocations.

1446
02:26:17,888 --> 02:26:27,488
So we require a valid Google ID token or OIDC token from the user before they can invoke a tool, before the agent can invoke a tool.

1447
02:26:28,302 --> 02:26:36,004
then we have another layer, which is OAuth scopes, OAuth 2.1 OIDC scopes per tool.

1448
02:26:36,288 --> 02:26:43,968
So if an agent needs to read something from a database, they have to have the specific permissions.

1449
02:26:44,110 --> 02:26:52,964
But if they're going to read operational database or making some changes to customers' records, they need a separate set of scopes.

1450
02:26:55,182 --> 02:27:10,256
This controls what the agent can like if a database user can log into your application, that doesn't fully secure yet, right?

1451
02:27:10,496 --> 02:27:14,256
Because what if customer A sees the data of customer B?

1452
02:27:14,416 --> 02:27:15,204
You don't want that.

1453
02:27:15,598 --> 02:27:17,924
For that, we have authenticated parameters.

1454
02:27:18,336 --> 02:27:33,284
So Toolbox uses OIDC tokens to pull in data like user ID or tenant IDs right from the OIDC token claims and plug them right into the SQL queries in a very predictable and predefined way.

1455
02:27:33,536 --> 02:27:37,456
So you cannot have customer A look into customer B's data.

1456
02:27:37,536 --> 02:27:39,076
They can only see their own data.

1457
02:27:39,536 --> 02:27:47,556
This is similar to what, or this is actually the same as what Andrew mentioned in the morning, parameterized secure values.

1458
02:27:48,142 --> 02:27:51,812
So PSVs are mostly using authenticated parameters.

1459
02:27:52,206 --> 02:27:53,764
Then we have secure parameters.

1460
02:27:54,096 --> 02:27:57,956
These are slightly different from the authenticated parameters.

1461
02:27:58,256 --> 02:28:11,076
These are not set given by the user, these are preset by the agentic application or us the developers before the agent logs in or the user logs in.

1462
02:28:12,046 --> 02:28:16,896
So we can set things like customer ID and stuff in a very predictable way.

